// Malachim, the Writing of the Angels.
//
// From the wing's research file malachim.verified.json. Where its fact-check
// left a point open, the later checks against the prints settle what they
// can: the 1533 caption, pages, signature and the count of twenty-three signs
// (two labelled Samech), and the Latin chapter heading, from
// the-books.verified.json (Library of Congress copy, page images; McGill copy,
// OCR); the III.29 ascription to Petrus Apponus from the same; the II.51 and
// III.30 passages and their 1651 pages from agrippa.verified.json. Every
// English quotation is J.F.'s (London, 1651), as keyed in EEBO-TCP A26565
// (CC0) and checked there, as are the 1651 spellings used for the names (the
// rows the notes cite are the printed page's, which the keying gets wrong
// where it leaves out Schin). Source
// URLs are only those the verified research files accept. The Würzburg
// shelfmark of the 1510 draft, which agrippa.verified.json left as not seen,
// is the-books.verified.json's (search-seen) and was seen again in review.
//
// The signs are traced from the table of 1533 in the Library of Congress copy
// (loc.gov 0278, the plate agrippa-angelic-1533-loc-malachim), checked against
// the McGill and Jung copies and compared with two copies of the English of
// 1651 (traced/malachim/glyphs.json, independently checked against the print).
// Each glyph is the traced path data unchanged, so the page draws it exactly
// as the tracer's specimen sheet does. The tracing also settled what the
// EEBO-TCP keying left open: the printed page of 1651 has Shin (labelled
// Schin), and both tables set Tau, Shin and the two Samechs after Nun. A
// letter's note carries only what the tracing tells a reader about that
// letter. The plate is the same page of the Library of Congress copy, whose
// repository states no known restriction.
