        author: 'Heinrich Cornelius Agrippa von Nettesheim (1486–1535)',
        date: 'July 1533 — the colophon reads “Anno M. D. XXXIII. Mense Iulio.”',
        year: 1533,
        place: '[Cologne]. The imprint is unsigned: the catalogues supply Johannes Soter as printer, the Library of Congress and Eton with a query, and Eton’s record notes Adams’s suggestion of Basel. Four or five issues dated 1533 are reported. Three copies were compared for this page: those of the Library of Congress and McGill, which share one setting of these pages, and Jung’s, which has another; the rest were not seen.',
        locator: 'Book III, chapter 30, “Alius characterizandi modus, à Cabalistis traditus”: in the Library of Congress copy the caption “Scriptura Malachim” stands at the foot of p. CCLXXIII (misprinted CCXLXXIII; signed z iii), under the Celestial’s table, and the table of twenty-three signs on p. CCLXXIIII, which it shares with the Passing of the River; the McGill copy has the same pages. In the English of 1651 the chapter is headed “CHAP. XXX. Another manner of making Characters, delivered by Cabalists.” (p. 439), and the table, “The writing called Malachim.”, is on p. 440.',
        notes: 'The earliest printing the sources document. Agrippa had sent a draft of the work to Trithemius, whose reply is dated 8 April 1510, but whether that draft already held this chapter and its tables is not known; Vittoria Perrone Compagni’s critical edition (Leiden: Brill, 1992), which takes account of the draft, is the place to settle it. A blog report (Cipher Mysteries, 2009) that the River alphabet of Panteo’s Voarchadumia (Venice, 1530) mixes signs from all three of Agrippa’s alphabets would, if true, put Malachim-like signs in print three years earlier; it is known only at second hand, and the plate has not been compared. Dates of 1531 confuse the book with the printings of that year, which hold Book I only.',
